\begin{aligned}
y-(-1)&=4(x-(1)), \\y&=4 x - 5.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=4\cdot 0+(-5), \\y&=-5.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(0,-5)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=- 2 x^{\frac{1}{2}} + 2, \\f'(x)&=- \frac{1}{x^{\frac{1}{2}}}=- \frac{1}{\sqrt{x}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(4)&=- \frac{1}{\sqrt{(4)}}=- \frac{1}{2}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(- \frac{1}{2}\right)}=2.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(4,-2)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-2)&=2(x-(4)), \\y&=2 x - 10.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=2x+(-10), \\x&=5.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $(5,0)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=x^{\frac{3}{2}} + 3, \\f'(x)&=\frac{3 x^{\frac{1}{2}}}{2}=\frac{3 \sqrt{x}}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(4)&=\frac{3 \sqrt{(4)}}{2}=3, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{(3)}=- \frac{1}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(4,11)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(11)&=- \frac{1}{3}(x-(4)), \\y&=- \frac{x}{3} + \frac{37}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=- \frac{1}{3}\cdot 0+\left(\frac{37}{3}\right), \\y&=\frac{37}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(0,\frac{37}{3}\right)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=x^{\frac{2}{3}} - 2, \\f'(x)&=\frac{2}{3 x^{\frac{1}{3}}}=\frac{2}{3 \sqrt[3]{x}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(1)&=\frac{2}{3 \sqrt[3]{(1)}}=\frac{2}{3}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)}=- \frac{3}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $(1,-1)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-(-1)&=- \frac{3}{2}(x-(1)), \\y&=- \frac{3 x}{2} + \frac{1}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $x$-axis, $y=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
0&=- \frac{3}{2}x+\left(\frac{1}{2}\right), \\x&=\frac{1}{3}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(\frac{1}{3},0\right)$.

Rewrite the radical or reciprocal using powers, then differentiate.

$$
\begin{aligned}
f(x)&=3 - \frac{1}{x}, \\f'(x)&=\frac{1}{x^{2}}=\frac{1}{x^{2}}.
\end{aligned}
$$

Evaluate the tangent slope and take its negative reciprocal to find the normal slope.

$$
\begin{aligned}
m_t=f'(2)&=\frac{1}{(2)^{2}}=\frac{1}{4}, \\m_n=-\frac{1}{m_t}&=-\frac{1}{\left(\frac{1}{4}\right)}=-4.
\end{aligned}
$$

Use point-slope form through $\left(2,\frac{5}{2}\right)$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y-\left(\frac{5}{2}\right)&=-4(x-(2)), \\y&=- 4 x + \frac{21}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

At the $y$-axis, $x=0$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
y&=-4\cdot 0+\left(\frac{21}{2}\right), \\y&=\frac{21}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

The intersection is $\left(0,\frac{21}{2}\right)$.
